{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794389058,"nonce":"NproXjBYEVlWpRQ4OWuYb97MVng=","title":"Callbacks have a layout but no runtime adapter","message":"hxn-abi defines `Callback {call, env, drop}` and unit-tests it against a Rust\ntrampoline. Neither runtime can build one yet, and tests/counter does not pass\na Haxe closure to Rust.\n\nWhat is missing:\n\n- HashLink: a trampoline that calls a `vclosure` through `hl_dyn_call_safe`\n  (so a Haxe exception does not longjmp across Rust frames), boxes arguments\n  to `vdynamic`, and keeps the closure alive with `hxn_hl::Root`.\n- hxcpp: a C++ trampoline that calls the `Dynamic` closure inside try/catch,\n  with `env` kept alive by `hx::GCAddRoot`, and `drop` removing the root.\n- A stored callback has to live in a `Handle\u003cT\u003e`, which requires `T: Send`\n  because a collector finalizes on any thread. `Root` is deliberately not\n  `Send`. A handle that owns a root also drops it from a finalizer, inside a\n  collection, where `hl_remove_root` takes the collector's lock. Whether that\n  is safe on stock HashLink and on Ash is unverified.\n- Calling back from a thread the VM does not know. hl_xidl's `attached`\n  registers the thread for the call; hxn-hl has no equivalent, and documents\n  every function as being for the calling thread only.\n\nThis also decides how a future is settled, so it is worth designing with the\nthree-state future result from the scope document.","files":null}]}